The reason the batch isn't disappearing automatically is because of the status mapping we just applied.
When you click "Approve", the database instantly locks the row and changes its status to QUEUED while it processes in the background. Because we told the backend to mask QUEUED as PENDING so the user doesn't see weird statuses, your frontend refreshes, sees the batch is still labeled as "PENDING", and puts it right back on the screen!
To fix this, we need to tell the **Pending Authorization API** to completely ignore QUEUED batches. This way, the moment you click approve, it instantly drops off your screen. If the background process fails, it will revert to PENDING and magically reappear for you to try again.
Here is the exact replacement for the getPendingBatchSummaries method in your **JournalRequestServiceImpl.java** file:
### Replace this method:
```java
    @Override
    public List<Map<String, Object>> getPendingBatchSummaries() {
        // FIX: Removed 'QUEUED' from the SQL IN clause.
        // Now, the moment a user clicks Approve/Reject, it locks into QUEUED in the database and instantly vanishes from their Pending screen.
        String sql = "SELECT BATCH_ID, CREATOR_ID, REQ_DATE, BATCH_REMARKS, TOTAL_ROWS, TOTAL_DEBIT, TOTAL_CREDIT, BATCH_STATUS, EXECUTOR_REMARKS " +
                "FROM JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER WHERE BATCH_STATUS IN ('PENDING', 'ERROR') ORDER BY REQ_DATE DESC";

        return jdbcTemplate.query(sql, (rs, rowNum) -> {
            Map<String, Object> map = new HashMap<>();
            map.put("batchId", rs.getString("BATCH_ID"));
            map.put("creatorId", rs.getString("CREATOR_ID"));
            map.put("requestDate", rs.getTimestamp("REQ_DATE"));
            map.put("commonBatchRemarks", rs.getString("BATCH_REMARKS"));
            map.put("requestCount", rs.getLong("TOTAL_ROWS"));
            map.put("totalDebit", rs.getBigDecimal("TOTAL_DEBIT"));
            map.put("totalCredit", rs.getBigDecimal("TOTAL_CREDIT"));
            
            // Strictly force the frontend to only see "PENDING"
            map.put("requestStatus", "PENDING");
            
            // Completely hide any error messages from the frontend UI
            map.put("errorMessage", null);
            
            return map;
        });
    }

```
By removing 'QUEUED' from the WHERE BATCH_STATUS IN (...) SQL query, your React frontend's automatic refresh will work perfectly again, immediately clearing the approved row from your view!
